    description: 'Marketed as the first 64-bit console, the Jaguar suffered from poor developer tools, a baffling 17-button controller, and a library of fewer than 70 games.',
    longDescription: 'Atari bet everything on the Jaguar, marketing it in America with the tagline "Do the math!" to emphasise its 64-bit claim over the 16-bit SNES and Genesis. In practice the hardware was hard to get the best from: bugs and unfinished development tools pushed many developers to lean on its Motorola 68000 rather than its custom chips, and third-party support never materialised. Only 63 licensed games were released over its lifespan. Tempest 2000 and Alien vs. Predator were genuinely excellent but could not carry the platform.\n\nThe console was designed by Flare Technology, the company formed by Martin Brennan and John Mathieson, and Atari cancelled its Panther project in 1991 to concentrate on it. IBM manufactured the Jaguar under a $500 million deal. It went on sale in test markets in New York and San Francisco on 23 November 1993 at $249.95, with a nationwide release following in 1994.\n\nSales never took off. Atari sold about 100,000 units by the end of 1994 and roughly 125,000 through 1995, with about 100,000 more left in inventory; the Jaguar CD add-on arrived in September 1995, too late to change anything. Atari abandoned the platform that November and merged with the disk-drive maker JTS in 1996. The story had two odd codas: in 1999 Hasbro Interactive released the Jaguar\'s patents into the public domain, opening the door to homebrew games, and the console\'s moulds were bought by a dental imaging developer and adapted to make a dental camera.',
