    description: 'An open-licence hardware platform launched at $699.99 — twice the price of its competitors — that never attracted the software library needed to justify the cost.',
    longDescription: 'Trip Hawkins, founder of Electronic Arts, created The 3DO Company to license a hardware design to multiple manufacturers. The concept was sound — one format, multiple makers — but the $699.99 launch price in 1993 was far above rival consoles. By the time prices dropped, the PlayStation and Saturn had arrived. The 3DO had some notable showcases (Road Rash, The Need for Speed) but most titles were multiplatform and better served elsewhere.\n\nThe hardware was designed by Dave Needle and RJ Mical of the New Technology Group, starting from a napkin outline in 1989, and The 3DO Company was spun off from EA in September 1991. Panasonic launched the first model, the FZ-1, in North America on 4 October 1993, and GoldStar and Sanyo later made their own versions. The company earned royalties on hardware and charged publishers only $3 per game, and Time named the 3DO its 1993 Product of the Year.\n\nThe weakness was built into the model: manufacturers had to make a profit on the hardware itself, so the console stayed expensive, falling only to $399.99. Only one game, Crash \'N Burn, was ready at launch, though later titles included Gex, Myst and Alone in the Dark. GoldStar dropped the system in early 1996 and production ended late that year. Estimates of total sales vary from about 750,000 to two million.',
