    longDescription: 'Designed by Nintendo R&D1 under Gunpei Yokoi, creator of the Game Boy, the Virtual Boy used red LED displays licensed from Reflection Technology, swept across the player\'s view by oscillating mirrors, to create a stereoscopic image. Users pressed their face into a visor while the unit sat on a tabletop, and players reported headaches, dizziness and eye strain. Nintendo discontinued the system within a year, after only 22 games were released — 14 of them in North America.\n\nThe design was a compromise from the start. Yokoi chose the red LED display over a full-colour headset project, and plans for head tracking were dropped in favour of a stationary tabletop unit because of concerns about motion sickness, children\'s eye health, injury and Japanese product liability law. Red LEDs were the cheapest option, used less battery power and gave the black backgrounds that sold the illusion of depth; every official game included an automatic pause that prompted players to take a break every 15 to 30 minutes.\n\nThe Virtual Boy launched in Japan on 21 July 1995 and in North America on 14 August at $179.95, backed by an estimated $25 million in promotion. It sold 770,000 units worldwide. Nintendo cut the US price to $99 in May 1996 and quietly ended production, in Japan in December 1995 and in North America in August 1996. Yokoi left Nintendo in August 1996; his departure was widely linked to the failure, though he had long planned to retire around that age and Nintendo called the timing coincidental.',
