    longDescription: 'Philips and Sony developed CD-i as an extension of the compact disc, and Philips built it into a family of standalone players aimed at home entertainment, education, training and point-of-sale displays. When an agreement with Nintendo to build a CD-ROM add-on for the Super NES never got past the prototype stage, Philips was left with the right to use Nintendo characters, and outside studios made four games with them — Hotel Mario and three Zelda titles. The results were notorious for their poor controls and bizarre animated cutscenes.\n\nThe format had a long gestation: development began in 1984 and CD-i was first announced in March 1986, but the first players did not reach North America until December 1991, followed by Japan in 1992 and Europe in July 1992. The US model initially sold for about $799 and was cut to $599 within a year. Its software ranged from edutainment and board-game adaptations to full-motion-video titles such as Burn:Cycle and Dragon\'s Lair, and over time games became its best-selling software.\n\nIt was never enough. Repeated delays let CD-ROM computers establish themselves first, full-motion video needed a separately sold cartridge, and the hardware was weak and expensive next to cheaper PCs. Philips claimed an installed base of 570,000 worldwide in October 1994; by 1996 it had largely abandoned the format, and it dropped the product line in 1998.',
