    longDescription: 'The 32X plugged into the Genesis cartridge slot and added two SH-2 processors — the same chips as the Saturn, at a lower clock speed — promising enhanced graphics and sound. Released in North America in November 1994, six months before the Saturn arrived there, the 32X undercut Sega\'s newer console and confused retailers and consumers about Sega\'s strategy. Only 40 games were released, six of them requiring the Sega CD as well, and the combination of Genesis, Sega CD and 32X became a symbol of Sega\'s hardware confusion in the mid-1990s.\n\nThe add-on began at CES in January 1994, when Sega president Hayao Nakayama demanded a quick answer to the Atari Jaguar. An upgraded Genesis was proposed, but Joe Miller of Sega of America argued for an add-on instead, to protect existing Genesis owners and cut costs. The project, codenamed Mars, was slowed by chip shortages and the language barrier between the US and Japan, and Sega itself described the result as a "transitional device" between the Genesis and the Saturn.\n\nIt launched in North America on 21 November 1994 at $159.99 with no pack-in game, and only six of the twelve promised launch titles. Early demand was real — about 500,000 US units sold by Christmas — but in Japan the Saturn had launched two weeks before the 32X, and in early 1995 Capcom and Konami cancelled their 32X projects. The price fell to $99 in September 1995, Sega discontinued the 32X in 1996 to focus on the Saturn, and remaining stock was cleared at $19.95.',
