    longDescription: 'The Saturn launched in Japan to strong sales and launched in North America four months ahead of schedule, at $399, with only six games available. Sony responded at the same E3 1995 by announcing that the PlayStation would cost $299. The Saturn\'s two SH-2 processors were powerful but complex — they shared a bus and could not access system memory at the same time — and analysts described the hardware as essentially 2D. Japanese developers thrived with it, producing landmark shoot-\'em-ups, RPGs and fighting games, but Western support dried up quickly.\n\nIn Japan the Saturn went on sale on 22 November 1994 at ¥44,800, and its first 200,000 units sold out on the first day, driven by Virtua Fighter, which sold almost one copy for every console. The surprise American launch in May 1995 upset retailers that had not been told in advance; KB Toys refused to stock the system at all.\n\nThe PlayStation outsold the Saturn two to one in 1996 and three to one in the US in 1997. Sega cancelled Sonic X-treme in early 1997, leaving the Saturn without a 3D Sonic platformer, and Bernie Stolar told E3 that year that "the Saturn is not our future". The console was discontinued in North America in March 1998 and in Japan in 2000, having sold 9.26 million units worldwide — 5.75 million of them in Japan and 1.8 million in the US. Many of its best Japanese games, such as Sakura Wars and Grandia, never received Western releases on the Saturn.',
