    description: 'The first handheld with a colour LCD screen, hardware sprite scaling, and backlit display — launched the same year as the Game Boy but overwhelmed by Nintendo\'s software library and battery life advantages.',
    longDescription: 'Designed at Epyx as the "Handy Game" and taken over by Atari when Epyx went bankrupt, the Lynx was technically ahead of the Game Boy in almost every hardware dimension: a backlit colour screen, hardware sprite scaling and a custom graphics chip. Several Lynx units could be linked for multiplayer games. But it ran on six AA batteries that lasted only four to five hours, and its library reached just 71 games against Nintendo\'s dominance with Tetris and Mario.\n\nIts designers, RJ Mical and Dave Needle, had worked on the original Amiga. The Lynx launched in North America in September 1989, five months after the Game Boy, at $179.99, and reached Europe and Japan in 1990. Its screen could be flipped for right- or left-handed play, and up to eight players could be linked reliably.\n\nThe Lynx sold about 500,000 units in the US in 1990 and an estimated 800,000 by late 1991. The smaller Lynx II followed in July 1991 at $99, with stereo headphone output, rubber grips and slightly longer battery life, but by then it also faced Sega\'s Game Gear, released in May 1991 for 0 less. Atari discontinued the Lynx in 1995.',
