    longDescription: 'The PC Engine, developed by Hudson Soft and manufactured by NEC, launched in Japan on 30 October 1987, sold 500,000 units in its first week and was at one point the best-selling console in the country. Its HuCard format, a thin credit-card cartridge, was innovative, and the CD-ROM² add-on of December 1988 made it the first console to use CD-ROM as a storage medium. In North America, branded as TurboGrafx-16, the launch was hampered by limited distribution and a small library, and Japan received dozens of exclusive titles — including Castlevania: Rondo of Blood — that never crossed the Pacific at the time.\n\nThe North American release in August 1989 came after the Sega Genesis was already on sale, with Keith Courage in Alpha Zones as a weak pack-in. NEC\'s costly redesign of the console for the American market, an overproduction of 750,000 units and a lack of third-party support all worked against it; 750,000 consoles had been sold there by March 1991. In mid-1992 Turbo Technologies, a joint venture of Hudson and NEC, took over the North American business, and launched the TurboDuo, which combined the console and CD drive, that October.\n\nIn Japan the story was very different: the PC Engine family had sold 10 million units by 1995, and NEC released several variants, including the more powerful SuperGrafx of December 1989, which received only five exclusive games. The console was discontinued in North America on 11 May 1994 and in Japan on 16 December 1994.',
