    description: 'A handheld promoted as a gaming PDA with internet connectivity and a touchscreen — none of which worked well — competing against the Game Boy Color with a poor game library and worse screen.',
    longDescription: 'Tiger Electronics marketed the Game.com as a cutting-edge handheld with a touchscreen, two cartridge slots, internet capabilities via a modem, and PDA features. In practice the screen was unlit and reflective and its games ran at slow frame rates, and the internet access was text-only and limited. Only twenty games were released, mostly made in-house by Tiger.\n\nThe Game.com went on sale in the US on 12 September 1997 at $69.95, and in the UK late that year at £79.99. Its monochrome screen was larger than the Game Boy\'s, and it shipped with a stylus for the touchscreen and built-in PDA tools — a phone directory, calculator and calendar — plus Solitaire, with Lights Out as the pack-in game. A 14.4 kbit/s modem added email and text-only web browsing, making it the first handheld games console with internet connectivity.\n\nTiger secured some big licences: Resident Evil 2, Duke Nukem 3D, Mortal Kombat Trilogy, Sonic Jam and Frogger all received versions. Critics liked the touchscreen, sound and PDA features but not the blurry screen or the library. A smaller, cheaper Pocket Pro followed in 1999 at $29.99, with one cartridge slot and no internet features beyond submitting high scores, and the line was discontinued in 2000 after selling fewer than 300,000 units.',
