    description: 'A GPS-equipped handheld whose executives turned out to have criminal pasts, which sold fewer than an estimated 25,000 units before its maker collapsed in 2006 with about £160 million of debt.',
    longDescription: 'Gizmondo Europe, part of Florida-based Tiger Telematics, launched the Gizmondo with ambitious features: GPS, a camera, GPRS connectivity, Bluetooth, and a planned "Smart Adds" model that would cut the price for buyers willing to see advertisements. The system had few games, a high price and poor distribution, and in October 2005 the Swedish newspaper Aftonbladet revealed that several of its executives had criminal pasts. Director Stefan Eriksson, linked to the "Uppsala mafia", crashed a Ferrari Enzo at an estimated 162 mph on a California highway weeks after the company was liquidated, and was later jailed and deported.\n\nThe device began as a GPS tracker for children called Gametraq before chairman Carl Freer turned it into a games console, renamed Gizmondo in 2004. It launched in the UK on 19 March 2005 at £229, or £129 for the Smart Adds version, and in the US on 22 October 2005 at $400. Only 14 games were released, 8 of them in the US, and about 30 more were cancelled. The advertising service never launched, and a widescreen revision announced just weeks before the US launch may have discouraged buyers from the existing model.\n\nThe company spent heavily on image, from a celebrity launch party in London to a Gizmondo-sponsored Ferrari at the 2005 24 Hours of Le Mans. Gizmondo Europe went into administration in January 2006 and was liquidated in February with about £160 million of debt, and liquidators could not account for about half of Tiger Telematics\' losses. GamePro called it the worst-selling handheld ever. A "Gizmondo 2" announced by Freer in 2007 never appeared.',
