    longDescription: 'Apple designed the Pippin as an open multimedia platform that it would license to other manufacturers rather than sell itself, built on a PowerPC 603 processor and a version of the Mac OS running from CD-ROM. Bandai made the main models: the ATMARK, released in Japan in March 1996 at ¥64,800, and the black @WORLD, released in the US in June 1996 at $599. The device was pitched as an internet appliance and a games machine at once, and shipped with a modem and a controller with a built-in trackball.\n\nAt that price it found almost no buyers. About 42,000 units were sold worldwide, 30,000 in Japan and 12,000 in the US, and most of its software was Japanese, with only a handful of titles released in North America. A second licensee, Katz Media, sold a version mainly for business uses in Europe and Canada.\n\nBandai halted production during merger talks with Sega, and when Steve Jobs returned to Apple in 1997 he ended the company\'s licensing of its technology to other manufacturers, which ended the Pippin concept with it. Bandai stopped production by mid-1997, and DayStar bought the remaining stock in 1998. PC World later ranked the @WORLD 22nd on its 2006 list of the worst tech products of all time.',
