    longDescription: 'The Sega Pico was designed for young children, aged about three to seven, and featured book-style cartridges called "Storyware" whose illustrated pages worked with a stylus, the "Magic Pen", and a drawing pad. It connected to a TV and played educational games covering counting, spelling, reading, music and colouring, many featuring licensed characters. In Japan it sold well and received a successor, the Advanced Pico Beena, in 2005; in North America it sold modestly.\n\nThe Pico was built on Sega Genesis hardware in a case designed to look like a laptop, but it had no screen of its own. It was released in Japan in June 1993 at ¥16,000, and in North America in November 1994 at $139. Its licensed titles included Disney\'s The Lion King and Winnie the Pooh as well as Sega\'s own Sonic the Hedgehog, and it earned some awards as a learning aid.\n\nSega discontinued the Pico in North America in early 1998, although Majesco re-released a version in August 1999 at $49.99. Sega claimed 3.4 million Pico consoles and 11.2 million cartridges sold worldwide as of 2005. The Beena added multiplayer, saved data and playtime limits, and Sega estimated it had sold 4.1 million units by 2010.',
