    description: 'Amstrad’s 8-bit console, launched into the teeth of the 16-bit era with a threadbare library and a cartridge-supply crisis, lasting barely six months on the market.',
    longDescription: 'Released in Britain, France, Spain and Italy in September 1990 for £99.99, the GX4000 was built on the architecture of Amstrad\'s CPC home computers, sharing it with the new CPC Plus range, and added hardware sprites, smooth scrolling and a 4,096-colour palette that the standard CPCs lacked. But its timing was catastrophic: it arrived against Sega\'s 16-bit Mega Drive, and Sega and Nintendo outspent it on both marketing and software. Most of its 27 games were ports or repackaged CPC titles with little or no improvement.\n\nThe console shipped with Burnin\' Rubber, a power pack and two controllers, and Amstrad backed it with a £20 million marketing budget. Early previews praised its graphics and design, but there was a simple problem with the software: its cartridges cost about £25, while the same games on cassette for the CPC cost as little as £3.99. In March 1991 a "cartridge crisis", blamed on cartridge manufacturing that could take months, starved it of new games.\n\nSales were weak from the start. Amstrad cut the price to £79.95 in March 1991, and by July some shops were selling it for £29.99. It was quietly discontinued in 1991, with sales often put at fewer than 15,000 units — a figure that is itself disputed. Its designer Cliff Lawson later argued that the hardware was "at least as good" as the SNES, and blamed the lack of games and money.',
