    description: 'The powerful but ill-fated successor to the Atari 2600, crippled by notoriously unreliable non-centering joysticks, a lack of backward compatibility, and the 1983 industry crash.',
    longDescription: 'Released in October 1982 to answer the ColecoVision and Intellivision, the Atari 5200 "SuperSystem" was genuinely capable hardware derived from Atari\'s 8-bit computers. Yet Atari sabotaged it with self-inflicted wounds. Its analog joysticks did not self-centre — the stick stayed wherever you left it instead of springing back to neutral — making precise play maddening and the controllers prone to failure. The console was not backward compatible with the enormous 2600 library at launch; an adapter came later but was designed only for the revised model. And it arrived just months before the video game crash of 1983, which gutted the entire North American console market.\n\nAtari\'s Advanced Technology Group began work on the console, codenamed PAM, in 1981. It was unveiled at CES in January 1982 as the "Supergame", renamed the Atari 5200 in June, and released at $299.95, though it was hard to find until January 1983. Besides the joystick, which combined the stick with a keypad and Start, Pause and Reset keys, Atari sold a Pro-Line Trak-Ball; a promised voice module never appeared.\n\nIn 1983 Atari released a cheaper two-port model at $199 with Pac-Man included, alongside the 2600 adapter, but the 5200 still sold poorly against its own predecessor. Atari announced its discontinuation in May 1984, at the same time as it introduced the 7800, after reported sales of just over a million units.',
