    description: 'A Genesis and 32X combined into one machine for under $200. Sega advertised it in 1995 and cancelled it that October, and the "prototype" in the photos was a non-working mock-up of wood and plastic.',
    longDescription: 'The 32X add-on had given the Genesis a second life as a 32-bit machine, but it meant a tower of hardware: a console, a mushroom-shaped adapter on top and a second power supply. The Neptune was Sega\'s plan to tidy that up by combining the Genesis and the 32X into a single unit. It appeared in Sega Magazine as early as March 1995, and Sega\'s official 1995 product catalogue presented it as the "Genesis 32X System", with a planned retail price of under $200 and a release planned for the winter.\n\nIt never arrived. In October 1995 Sega cancelled the Neptune, concerned that another piece of hardware would dilute its marketing focus on the Saturn, and that a sub-$200 hybrid would sit uncomfortably close to its own 32-bit console. By then the 32X itself was failing commercially, and the Saturn had launched in the West that May.\n\nThe Neptune is often described as a prototype, but the unit shown in promotional material was a non-functional mock-up built from wood and plastic to illustrate the intended design. Its legend outlived it: in April 2001 Electronic Gaming Monthly ran an April Fools\' story claiming Sega had discovered a warehouse of unsold Neptunes.',
