    description: 'A $30 headset that projected a red LCD game onto a mirror in front of one eye. Tiger launched it in 1995, the same year as the Virtual Boy, and it is routinely ranked among the worst consoles ever made.',
    longDescription: 'Tiger Electronics, known for its inexpensive handheld LCD games, unveiled the R-Zone at the American International Toy Fair in February 1995 and released it later that year. The original model, the R-Zone Headgear, was a head-mounted unit with a separate controller, sold for $29.99. Each game cartridge contained its own transparent LCD screen; the headset shone light through it and projected the image onto a mirrored surface held in front of one of the player\'s eyes. The display showed only dark red.\n\nThe idea borrowed the excitement around virtual reality in the mid-1990s — the same year Nintendo launched its own red, head-level Virtual Boy — without any of the technology. The R-Zone was not stereoscopic or immersive: it was a simple LCD game with a fixed, pre-printed screen, viewed through a mirror. Tiger nonetheless secured major licences, and the library included Batman Forever, Virtua Fighter, Mortal Kombat 3, Star Wars games and The Lost World: Jurassic Park.\n\nTiger revised the format repeatedly. The R-Zone SuperScreen of 1996 projected the image onto a larger screen and allowed colour, the DataZone added personal organiser functions, and the R-Zone XPG of 1997 became a conventional handheld. None succeeded, and the line was discontinued in 1997. A Chicago Tribune reviewer called it a waste of time and money, citing the uncomfortable headband and hard-to-see display, and later critics have consistently placed it among the worst consoles ever released.',
